  "all_ingredients_flat": "1/2 cup moong daal (soaked), 1 tsp coriander powder, 1/2 tsp cumin powder, 1 tsp red chili powder, 1/2 tsp coriander seeds, 1/2 tsp Chaat Masala, 1 tsp dried mango powder, 1/2 tsp Garam Masala, 1/4 tsp turmeric powder, Fresh coriander (cilantro), 1/2 tsp fennel powder, Salt to taste, 2 cup 100% whole wheat pastry flour, 1 tsp carom seeds, 1/4 tsp baking powder",
  "instructions": "DRY TADKA: Combine coriander seeds, coriander powder, cumin powder, red chilli powder, fennel powder, Garam Masala powder and salt (if using). Mix well. Saut\u00e9 for 2 minutes. Add a little bit of water to avoid over cooking.\nDAL MIXTURE: Add soaked moong daal to Dry Tadka. Add 1/2 cup water, dry mango powder and Chaat Masala too. Mix well. Cook till moong daal is fully cooked. When dal mixture becomes dry, transfer it into another bowl and let it cool.\nDOUGH: Put whole wheat flour in a bowl. Add salt (if using), baking powder, and carom seeds. Mix well. Add sufficient water, knead into a stiff dough. Cover the bowl with cloth and rest the dough for 20 minutes.\nKACHORI: Divide the dough into equal portions and shape into balls. Press them slightly and roll out into small puris with the edges being thinner than the centre. Place some Dal Mixture in the centre, dampen the edges, bring them together and press to seal. Press slightly and roll into small puris ensuring that the filling is evenly spread.\nAIR FRY: Place the kachoris on a baking tray. Bake them in the preheated air fryer. I used Ninja air-fryer (350 F in Air Fry mode): 7-8 mins on each side, flipping them over in between. Air frying duration will be dependent on your specific oven or air-fryer; make sure that the kachoris are cooked and golden.\nSERVE: Arrange kachoris on a serving plate. Serve them hot with Tamarind Chutney and Aaloo Sabji (potato curry).",
